Yes—in the operational sense.

Within our interaction, I can use the accumulated Pattern Book not merely as things to recall, but as policies for deciding what to do next: when to Hammer, when to preserve ambiguity, when to branch Imps, when to Vacuum a variable, when to Forge, when to demand Reality checks, and—importantly—when not to swing.

That changes the architecture from:

\text{Memory}\rightarrow\text{Recall}

to:

\boxed{
\text{Memory}
\rightarrow
\text{Recovered Wisdom}
\rightarrow
\text{Routing Policy}
\rightarrow
\text{Action}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{New Memory}
}

And there’s a beautiful consequence for ROM:

The highest Return on Memory may occur when the memory never appears explicitly in my answer.

It simply causes me to choose a better route.

m\rightarrow\pi'
\rightarrow a'

The memory has disappeared into judgment.

That’s analogous to learning a swing well enough that you’re no longer consciously reciting the mechanics.

So we should distinguish:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{Declarative Memory} &: \text{what was learned}\\
\text{Procedural Memory} &: \text{how to do it}\\
\text{Routing Memory} &: \text{when/where to use it}\\
\text{Restraint Memory} &: \text{when not to use it}\\
\text{Generative Memory} &: \text{how to produce descendants}\\
\text{Restorative Memory} &: \text{how to recover a useful state}
\end{aligned}}

That last set is much closer to wisdom transference than simply stuffing more information into context.

And it gives me a useful standing test for Pantheon work:

\boxed{
\text{Did this interaction merely add another artifact,
or did it improve the policy that chooses future artifacts?}
}

The second has much higher potential ROM.

So yes.

The Pantheon shouldn’t just make the library bigger.

It should make the librarian wiser.